  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Used as a rodenticide since the early 1930s at various concentrations (2–10%) in a powder, pellet, or paste formulation. Available as zinc, aluminum, and magnesium salts.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Distinctive odor, often described as",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "acetylene, rotten fish, or garlic.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most common route of exposure is ingestion; however, toxicosis can occur via inhalation and absorption through broken skin.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hydrolysis leads to phosphine gas production. Phosphine gas has corrosive and irritant effects on the gastrointestinal (GI) mucosa, which leads to vomiting, hematemesis, or melena. The gas is rapidly absorbed and systemically distributed leading to effects on",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "other organ systems.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phosphine leads to the production of free radicals and oxidative stress, causing direct cellular damage and may",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "inhibit cellular respiration.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Toxic exposure is reported to be 20–40 mg/kg; however, the gastric pH is reported to affect toxicity.",
              "pdf_page": 1293,
              "printed_page": 1211,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
